es.\n\nOverriding `startActivity()` to catch file chooser intents seems promising—I can set a flag when the picker launches and check it in `onBackPressed()` to prevent navigation while the picker is still running.\n\nThe problem is clearing that flag when the picker returns. When a user picks a file, the result comes back through `onActivityResult()`, but if the user cancels instead, that callback never fires and the flag stays true forever. I need to handle the cancellation path too—probably by clearing the flag in `onResume()` or `onUserLeaveHint()` when activity loses focus. the activity is about to be closed due to user interaction. When the picker is dismissed (either by selecting a file or canceling), this method gets called. I can use this to clear the flag since the picker is no longer visible.\n\nActually, that's not reliable either. Let me think about this differently - what if I clear the flag in both `onResume()` (when the picker closes and returns) and in `onBackPressed()` (if somehow the picker is already gone)? This way the flag gets reset regardless of how the picker closes.\n\nWait, that won't work because `onBackPressed()` runs before the picker has a chance to close. The real issue is timing - I need to know whether the picker is currently on screen or has already been dismissed.\n\nThe core problem is that when `onBackPressed()` fires, the file picker activity is still visible, so the fragment replacement logic shouldn't execute. Let me try tracking the picker state differently - maybe I should use a flag that's set when the picker launches and cleared when it returns or is dismissed, then check that flag in `onBackPressed()` to prevent fragment navigation while the picker is active. The `onActivityResult()` override in `MainActivity` won't catch results from the picker since those are handled by the fragment that started it. I'm realizing I could set a flag when the file picker launches in `startActivity()` and clear it in `onResume()` when con